  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nDrier air continues to work its way in overnight tonight. This \nbrings dew points down into the 50s and low 60s, allowing temps \nto follow and bring a much cooler night than the last several. \nLows bottom out in the upper 50s to near 60 for most interior \nlocation, while the coast holds onto some low to mid 60s.\n\nA seasonably warm day is expected tomorrow with widespread \nhighs in the mid 80s. A sea breeze does knock temps back along \nthe coast by the afternoon hours. Dew points will mainly be in \nthe low 60s tomorrow, keeping heat indices in step with the \nactual air temps.\n\nA cold front approaches during the day tomorrow. A weak low \ndevelops along this front and moves through southern Quebec late\ntomorrow and tomorrow night. This likely brings a round of \nshowers and storms in from the west by late tomorrow, and moreso\ntomorrow evening. The most widespread activity for these will \nbe across the north and near the Canadian border, with chances \ndropping off toward the coast. A strong storm can't be ruled out\nearly in the evening, but otherwise the main focus would be for\nheavy rainfall on any spots that see a couple storms move over \nthe same areas. This front then moves offshore by late in the \nnight, with clearing skies following by daybreak Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe weather looks relatively benign Tuesday into next weekend\nwith cooler and drier airmasses moving in from time to time. The\nbest chance of showers and thunderstorms looks to be late\nWednesday into Thursday based on a multi-model consensus.\nOtherwise mainly dry weather is expected with highs generally in\nthe 70s in the mountains to lower 80s to mid 80s at lower \nelevations Tuesday and Wednesday with a cooler temperatures \nthereafter.",
